#. type: Title #
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Quantum machine learning {#quantum-machine-learning}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Quantum machine learning (QML) is an emerging field that combines quantum computing and machine learning to explore new ways of analyzing data, \n"
"recognizing patterns, and solving complex computational problems. It applies concepts from quantum mechanics to develop quantum algorithms and machine\n"
"learning models that can run on quantum computers or integrate quantum and classical computing.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Research in QML includes quantum neural networks, quantum kernel methods, and hybrid quantum-classical algorithms, which use classical computers to \n"
"optimize models while quantum processors perform specific computations. Although QML might offer advantages for certain tasks, practical benefits over \n"
"classical machine learning remain an active area of research.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A common workflow trains a model mostly on classical hardware, and then uses"
" a quantum processor for a targeted subroutine, such as optimizing the "
"diversity of an ensemble of learners or evaluating a quantum kernel, where "
"quantum effects might provide an advantage over a purely classical approach."
" With this hybrid structure, you can apply quantum resources to the part of "
"the problem where they can have the most effect, while keeping most of a "
"machine learning pipeline, such as data preprocessing and large-scale "
"training, on classical infrastructure. The application functions on this "
"page follow this pattern: one for classification tasks, and one for feature "
"extraction.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "<span id=\"application-functions\"></span>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Application functions for quantum machine learning {#application-functions-"
"for-quantum-machine-learning}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The following quantum functions apply this hybrid approach to machine "
"learning tasks, and use quantum hardware at different points of a "
"pipeline:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Singularity Machine Learning - Classification** solves binary "
"classification problems. You provide your own training data, and the "
"function classically trains an ensemble of learners, then uses a quantum "
"algorithm to optimize that ensemble for diversity and generalization, before"
" returning predictions on new data.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Rimay Quantum Feature Extraction** adds a preprocessing step to an "
"existing machine learning pipeline. You provide classical tabular data, and "
"the function returns quantum-transformed features generated on quantum "
"hardware. You can use these features on their own, or combine them with your"
" original features, to train a classical model of your choice.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition type=\"note\" title=\"Notes\">\n"
"IBM Quantum&reg; Functions are a beta feature available only to IBM Quantum Premium Plan, Flex Plan, and On-Prem (through the IBM Quantum Platform API) Plan users. Refer to [Set up IBM Quantum Functions](https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/functions) for instructions on getting access.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""

